al  course I once heard the professor  richard fineman giving that the  successful scientist must know when to  discard the extraneous results and some  of that mindset came into shaping some  of our choice  this year and other is a work that goes  back a while to Thomas Kuhn who did very  interesting things on how linguistics  colors perception which i think is  actually at play in both scenarios that  I'll share with you and that one is what  we see can be influenced by what we've  seen and understood before and another  one from a fellow who I was able to  contract as a coach for the first outing  of health information exchange who  commented that innovation is not the  problem it's clearing the mind of the  old ideas so that the new thought can  emerge and yet another one that I think  is important in healthcare where a lot  of it is all expensive and big money  which is the late professor CK Prahalad  a colleague of his pointed me to this  work and the idea that part of  envisioning a successful future that's  inclusive will be doing things in a way  that with profitable service embraces  the unfortunate the unserved and the  many billions around the world who need  these good solutions and good  information but don't necessarily have  the kind of resource we have to afford  it so the first one looks at clinical  practice guidelines we went to the  digital patient chart started years ago  came very quickly lots of money invested  after that process including the  definition of the certified electronic  health record came we had Affordable  Care a political compromise with  affordable care it became important to  use clinical practice guidelines  something in the field of medicine that  professors would develop and as part of  training the medical students on  differential diagnosis would help them  know when to look at a clinical practice  guideline which would be a template for  dealing with a particular condition and  the clinical practice guideline would  give them some rule ins when to use it  rule outs when not to use it some care  selections and some follow-up steps and  those would be typically printed there's  been projects in the recent time where  working under a contract  with clinical practice guidelines they  would be laminated and the physician  could refer to them and then their  encounter note which would be quite a  lot of typing would go into the digital  system as a type note easily five  hundred a thousand keystrokes and what  we did was just produced a simple  Wolfram language script deployed with  the CDF player to structure these and  that meant that even though the system  that they're running a very expensive  digital system does not no clinical  practice guidelines you could use the  CDF make the selection of how the  particular guideline is used for the  patient paste it in and proceed along  the way  the traditional approach would be to  spend into the millions of dollars to  modify the digital system and attempt to  put the pieces of each guideline within  the digital system which can work and  people would make that investment if  they had the money and they would then  say okay and using our digital system we  follow the guidelines but then there'd  be the tiny thing can you track the use  of the guidelines and when the  guidelines are decomposed and spread  throughout the digital system there's  often not the reporting to be able to  know that whereas a clever thing if you  use a CDF and the consumption of a  guideline that may have a few dozen  steps is just a yes/no which of the  steps in the guideline were selected  it's a tiny little bit of data that can  be safely stored in a database or  anywhere that then reports across to all  the encounters use of the guideline and  it's also interesting that if you have a  typical enterprise structure of a health  system with the hospital and their  digital system many medical offices in  their digital system which could be  dozens of different choices that each  office would make what digital system  would they like  with an encounter note each time the  physician works with the patient use of  a CDF script allows across the whole  environment a uniform way to just track  in a which guideline and what elements  are used or not used and why at each  encounter so the question does it  actually work  the folks who used it and loved it were  uncomfortable with me sharing any detail  ultimately the health system they were  in decided to invest the big millions of  dollars and script all the guidelines  into the systems and give access to this  common system to many many physicians at  no cost to the physician which is an  accounting problem for the health system  but just kind of beyond our scope today  but we did find that it worked and it  worked very successfully and we're  considering after this conference  offering the capability in a general way  and we believe that we'll be able to do  that at whatever cost that the hospital  would like in fact our initial thought  was to tell them they can pick you know  any price scheme they want pay our  nonprofit half for the service and if  they decide they want to give it away  that'll be ok but if they call and ask  questions we might charge them so that's  one where we've got some experience I  wish I could show you more about it but  that has issues happy to discuss further  but we did demonstrate that the cdf  worked to augment the digital patient  chart the next thing that came along  this spring was CMS the group over  Medicare and Medicaid said we'd like to  demonstrate some explainable AI that  helps and that was a very nice challenge  they put money behind it and I said  we'll even provide the limited data set  of healthcare claims that you can use to  provide a solution so we were wondering  where would we look for a solution  domain and with some of the chief  medical officers which our nonprofit  seems to collect we state our mission  and people show up 